  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.1. Increased capacity of the member States to ensure respect for, promote and realize fundamental principles and rights at work</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The national strategy for child labour was revised by the Ministry of labour in consultation with the social partners and formally adopted in December 2021. The entire strategy was fully revised based on the findings of the 2016 National Child Labour Survey, the 2017 Juvenile Law, and the findings of the ILO Global Estimates for Child Labour Report 2020. The National Council for Family Affairs (NCFA) revised the procedures for child labour case management to include roles and responsibilities, and the revised procedures were adopted in December 2021. The ILO is a member of the committee responsible for updating the national strategy for child labour. As a member of the committee, the ILO provided technical comments on the prevention and protection section. As a member of the technical committee responsible on reviewing the Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs), the ILO provided technical feedback on the SOPs, trained government officials on the final product, and piloted the SOPs in the field.</narrative>
   </description>
